
### New guides, September 2026

- [The Texas Criminal Statute of Limitations](https://www.barrettcrimelaw.com/blog/texas-criminal-statute-of-limitations/): It depends entirely on the crime. Under Tex. Code Crim. Proc. art. 12.01, most felonies must be indicted within 3 years, but named offenses get longer: 5 years for theft, robbery and family violence assault, 7 years for most fraud...
- [Aggravated Robbery in Texas](https://www.barrettcrimelaw.com/blog/aggravated-robbery-texas/): Robbery plus one of three facts. Robbery under Tex. Penal Code §29.02 is theft where the person causes bodily injury or threatens or places another in fear of imminent injury or death, a second degree felony.
- [Evading Arrest in Texas](https://www.barrettcrimelaw.com/blog/evading-arrest-texas/): Fleeing on foot from an officer trying to lawfully arrest or detain you is a Class A misdemeanor under Tex. Penal Code §38.04, up to a year in jail.
- [Grand Juries and Indictments in Texas](https://www.barrettcrimelaw.com/blog/grand-jury-indictment-texas/): An indictment is the written accusation of a grand jury, presented to a court, charging a person with an offense (Tex. Code Crim. Proc. art. 21.01). Under art.
- [What Does a Criminal Defense Lawyer Cost in Texas?](https://www.barrettcrimelaw.com/blog/criminal-defense-lawyer-cost-texas/): There is no standard price, and any site quoting one exact number is guessing. What is standard is the structure: most Texas criminal defense work is priced as a flat fee by stage, set by four things: the charge level, whether the...

